---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../utils/paths";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method | Monohanzi"
  description="How Monohanzi presents components, evidence, and uncertainty."
>
  <main class="text-page">
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="brand" href={withBase("/")}>Monohanzi</a>
      <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href={withBase("/")}>Explore</a>
        <a href={withBase("/method/")} aria-current="page">Method</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <article class="editorial-page">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>Useful structure, honestly labeled.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Monohanzi helps you see a character’s internal structure without
        pretending every visible piece has one simple historical story.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2>What “component” means here</h2>
        <p>
          Component is our broad word for a useful region of a glyph. A
          component may contribute meaning, suggest sound, act as the dictionary
          radical, preserve history, or simply provide a practical visual region.
          Those jobs are shown separately on each page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How to read confidence</h2>
        <dl class="definition-list">
          <div>
            <dt>High</dt>
            <dd>The form or role is well supported and straightforward.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Medium</dt>
            <dd>The explanation is useful but needs historical or visual qualification.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Low</dt>
            <dd>The analysis is disputed or deliberately tentative.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The five-page prototype</h2>
        <p>
          This first collection intentionally tests five different cases: a
          meaning-based pairing, a semantic–phonetic character, an enclosure, a
          compressed historical form, and a modern shape that should only be
          split visually. The source notes on each page make the current evidence
          visible while the editorial method develops.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Glyph sources</h2>
        <p>
          The target pipeline uses
          <a href="https://github.com/Connum/hanzivg">Connum/HanziVG</a>
          as the first source to inspect, followed by technical and editorial
          review. The current five glyphs use HanziVG’s original stroke paths
          unchanged. Monohanzi only removes the stroke-number layer and adapts
          the existing groups, IDs, and accessibility metadata so those paths
          can be highlighted in place.
        </p>
        <p><a href={withBase("/credits/")}>Read asset and license notes →</a></p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
